From 6ea2f09529e9c15d8061e063f82c18717b7f47df Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 01:22:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09385/11362] Flag substrates with no duration floor; stop
 timing the argument list

Two defects found auditing exp6478's complete_positive energy claim.

1. THE GATE HAD A HOLE. `duration_floor_for_artifact` walks a chain of substrate
recognizers and returns None when none match; `check_duration_vs_claim` then
returns before applying any floor. So an artifact could declare a substrate the
linter has never been taught, carry any duration at all, and be reported clean.
The guard's pattern list was narrower than the concept it protects.

Measured over the 5,691-artifact corpus: 720 artifacts declare a substrate, are
not precondition-blocked, and receive NO floor. 237 of those ran in under one
second. exp6478 is how it surfaced -- its
`exact_solver_held_candidate_selection_no_llm` matched nothing.

The fix emits SUBSTRATE_HAS_NO_DURATION_FLOOR at WARN, not CRITICAL. Most of the
720 are honest work under an unrecognized name, and a critical flag would
retroactively quarantine them. The point is that an unchecked declaration should
be VISIBLE, so a floor gets added, instead of silence reading as approval.

Note this same bug class was already fixed once, on 2026-07-30, for the ARC
live-agent no-LLM substrate alone. That was a one-substrate patch of a general
problem. This generalizes it.

2. FOUR SCRIPTS TIMED THEIR OWN ARGUMENT LIST. They read
`duration_s=max(time.monotonic() - start, 0.0001)` as an ARGUMENT to
build_artifact, so elapsed time was evaluated BEFORE build_artifact ran any of
the work it was meant to time. The stored value was always exactly the 0.0001
floor whatever the real runtime, and duration_s' own declared principle -- that
wall time catches a comparison which skipped the expensive path -- could never
hold. Now the artifact is built first and the real elapsed time stamped onto it.

Verified: the new flag fires on exp6478 and stays silent on an artifact with a
recognized substrate.

